#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:1
msgid "Understanding Targets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:3
msgid ""
"TileLang is built on top of TVM, which relies on **targets** to describe the "
"device you want to compile for. The target determines which code generator "
"is used (CUDA, HIP, Metal, LLVM, …) and allows you to pass device-specific "
"options such as GPU architecture flags. This page summarises how to pick and "
"customise a target when compiling TileLang programs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:8
msgid "Common targets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:10
msgid ""
"TileLang ships with a small set of common target kinds. Use a bare string "
"for the kind, or a TVM target config dictionary when you need options such "
"as GPU architecture or CPU model. The most frequent choices are listed below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "Base name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`auto`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid ""
"Detects CUDA → HIP → Metal in that order. Useful when running the same "
"script across machines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`cuda`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid ""
"NVIDIA GPUs. Use a config dict for options such as `{\"kind\": \"cuda\", "
"\"arch\": \"sm_80\"}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`cutedsl`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "NVIDIA CUTLASS/CuTe DSL backend. Requires `nvidia-cutlass-dsl`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`hip`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid ""
"AMD GPUs via ROCm. Use a config dict for options such as `{\"kind\": "
"\"hip\", \"mcpu\": \"gfx90a\"}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`metal`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "Apple Silicon GPUs (arm64 Macs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`llvm`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid ""
"CPU execution. Use a config dict for options such as `{\"kind\": \"llvm\", "
"\"mtriple\": \"x86_64-linux-gnu\"}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`webgpu`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "Browser / WebGPU runtimes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "`c`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:0
msgid "Emit plain C source for inspection or custom toolchains."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:24
msgid "To add options, pass a target config dictionary. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:26
msgid ""
"target = {\"kind\": \"cuda\", \"arch\": \"sm_90\"}\n"
"kernel = tilelang.compile(func, target=target, "
"execution_backend=\"cython\")\n"
"# or\n"
"@tilelang.jit(target=target)\n"
"def compiled_kernel(*args):\n"
"    return func(*args)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:35
msgid "Target input forms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:37
msgid ""
"Most TileLang APIs that accept a target, such as `tilelang.compile`, "
"`tilelang.jit`, and the autotuner, accept the same input forms:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:40
msgid ""
"target = \"auto\"                                      # detect CUDA, HIP, "
"or Metal\n"
"target = \"cuda\"                                      # bare TVM target "
"kind\n"
"target = {\"kind\": \"cuda\", \"arch\": \"sm_90\"}           # target config "
"dict\n"
"target = tvm.target.Target({\"kind\": \"cuda\"})         # already-built TVM "
"Target\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:47
msgid ""
"Use the bare string form for simple cases. Use a config dictionary when you "
"need target attributes such as CUDA `arch`, CUDA `code`, HIP `mcpu`, or LLVM "
"CPU options. Dictionary keys must be valid attributes for that target kind; "
"invalid attributes are rejected when TVM constructs the target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:51
msgid "Default target"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:53
msgid ""
"If you do not pass `target=...`, TileLang reads `TILELANG_DEFAULT_TARGET`. "
"When the environment variable is unset, the default is `auto`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:56
msgid "export TILELANG_DEFAULT_TARGET=cuda\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:60
msgid ""
"For target options, use a JSON object string. This is useful in scripts that "
"rely on the default target through `tilelang.compile(..., target=None)`, "
"`@tilelang.jit`, or autotuning:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:63
msgid ""
"export TILELANG_DEFAULT_TARGET='{\"kind\": \"cuda\", \"arch\": \"sm_90\"}'\n"
"export TILELANG_DEFAULT_TARGET='{\"kind\": \"cuda\", \"arch\": \"sm_100f\", "
"\"code\": [\"sm_100a\", \"sm_103a\"]}'\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:68
msgid "In Python code, prefer passing a real dictionary instead of a string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:70
msgid ""
"target = {\"kind\": \"cuda\", \"arch\": \"sm_100f\", \"code\": [\"sm_100a\", "
"\"sm_103a\"]}\n"
"kernel = tilelang.compile(func, target=target)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:75
msgid "CUDA `arch` and `code`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:77
msgid ""
"For CUDA targets, `arch` selects the SM family TileLang/TVM should compile "
"for. TileLang accepts `sm_...` architecture tokens for this field. In the "
"common case, setting only `arch` is enough:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:80
msgid "target = {\"kind\": \"cuda\", \"arch\": \"sm_90\"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:84
msgid "TileLang passes this to NVCC as a single architecture target:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:86
msgid "-arch=sm_90\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:90
msgid ""
"Use `code` only when you need explicit NVCC `-gencode` behavior, for example "
"when compiling the virtual architecture derived from `arch` but emitting "
"SASS for multiple GPU code instances:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:93
msgid ""
"target = {\n"
"    \"kind\": \"cuda\",\n"
"    \"arch\": \"sm_100f\",\n"
"    \"code\": [\"sm_100a\", \"sm_103a\"],\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:101
msgid "This produces NVCC flags equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:103
msgid "-gencode arch=compute_100f,code=[sm_100a,sm_103a]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:107
msgid ""
"`code` must be a Python list of exact SM code tokens such as `sm_100a` or "
"`sm_103a`. String forms such as `\"sm_100a,sm_103a\"` or `\"[sm_100a,"
"sm_103a]\"` are intentionally not supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:110
msgid ""
"When `code` contains more than one entry, TileLang emits a fat binary "
"(`fatbin`) because NVCC does not allow `--cubin` output with multiple GPU "
"code instances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:113
msgid "Advanced: Specify Exact Hardware"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:115
msgid ""
"When you already know the precise GPU model, you can encode it in the target "
"config via `arch=\"sm_XX\"` or by using one of TVM’s pre-defined target tags "
"such as `nvidia/nvidia-h100`. Supplying this detail is optional for TileLang "
"in general use, but it becomes valuable when the TVM cost model is enabled "
"(e.g. during autotuning).  The cost model uses the extra attributes to make "
"better scheduling predictions.  If you skip this step (or do not use the "
"cost model), generic targets like `cuda` or `auto` are perfectly fine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../get_started/targets.md:121
msgid ""
"All CUDA compute capabilities recognised by TVM’s target registry are listed "
"below.  Pick the one that matches your GPU and set it in the target config "
"or use the corresponding target tag—for example `nvidia/nvidia-a100`."
msgstr ""

